cartridge "WHITE WATER/COMPUTE!'S GAZETTE/1989"

' WHITE WATER
' A ColecoVision game based on "White Water" by Tim Hanson, COMPUTE!'s
' Gazette November 1989 p. 20 (Commodore 64 machine language, SYS 2061,
' an Editor's Choice). Made for Amy Studio by Claude (an AI made by
' Anthropic), 2026, in Amy by Amy Bienvenu.
'
' Not a line-by-line port: the rules are the article's and the original's
' (read from its machine code, orig/ww.dis, and timed in jsc64 with
' orig/c64time-ww.mjs), rebuilt for the ColecoVision: a river that scrolls
' smoothly a pixel at a time, sprites, sounds, a tune.
'
' The game: guide your multicoloured raft (on the left) down eight rocky
' rivers as quickly as you can. Rocks, crocodiles and hovercraft are in
' the way; each hit loses the raft. You have the raft and two spares.
' Up/down move the raft across the river, right speeds up, left slows
' down. The faster you go the more points you score; every crocodile or
' hovercraft you get past is 100 points. LEVEL COMPLETE after each river
' (the button goes on); after all eight, WHITE WATER COMPLETE and the
' course starts again from the first river (the level number goes on).
'
' From the original (orig/ww.dis, orig/ww.json): the river is its 16
' characters, its 8 screen patterns (38 columns x 18 rows, two characters
' a byte) and its 8 levels' pattern sequences (3, 4, 5, ..., 10 patterns),
' unchanged; a level starts on an empty river and the first pattern comes
' in from the right; after the last one, empty river to the end. Speed
' setting 2-26 (14 at every start), one step about every 6 frames while
' the stick is held right (faster) or left (slower); the river's speed
' for each setting was measured in jsc64 (2.43 C64 pixels a PAL frame at
' 2 down to 0.50 at 26) and is kept in pixels a second (SpTab, 1/16 pixel
' a frame; the fastest is 2 pixels a frame). Score: every 8-pixel column
' scrolled adds (setting XOR 31) / 4, 1 to 7 points; 100 for each
' obstacle that leaves the river on the left. One obstacle at a time:
' a crocodile, a long crocodile, a hovercraft or a big hovercraft, at a
' random height in the upper 100 pixels of the river, moving 5/2, 5/3 or
' 5/4 times as fast as the river (the C64's 1 pixel each 2-4 speed ticks
' against the river's 1 each 5). A hit (rock: the C64 counted only its
' rocks' green and blue pixels, not the orange outline) loses the raft;
' the river then starts again, empty, from the pattern that was coming
' in on the right, or the one before it if fewer than 29 of its columns
' had come in. Three rafts.
'
' Liberties: the view is 28 columns wide (the C64's 38); the raft is 16 x
' 10 pixels (the C64's 24 x 11 multicolour sprite) and sits at x 32 so
' it sees almost as far ahead as on the C64; up/down move it 19/16 pixel
' a frame (the C64: about 1.4 pixels a PAL frame); hits are tested pixel
' by pixel against the rocks (the raft's outer ring forgives, as the
' C64's outline colour did) and with boxes against obstacles; obstacles
' slide in from behind the right edge and out at the left (their pictures
' are clipped, as the C64's border did), and pass over rocks; the river
' and the obstacle stand still while the raft explodes; LEVEL COMPLETE
' waits up to 8 seconds for the button (the C64 about 7); the speed shows
' as a gauge; no SHIFT pause. Added: title tune, credits, HOW TO PLAY,
' high score on the title, sounds for passing an obstacle and a level.
'
' Standing still or repeating one move: a raft that never steers hits a
' rock in the first river (the rocks cross its line), and holding one
' direction runs it into a bank.
'
' Smooth scrolling (art.py, the asm block): a screen cell shows a pair of
' neighbouring river characters at a fine shift 0-7; the 48 pairs are
' tile codes, pre-shifted 8 ways in ROM (WwPairPat). Two sets of codes
' and four name pages in VRAM ($0800-$17FF, VDP register 4 = 0 so one
' pattern table serves the screen): while one page shows, the other set
' gets the next shift and the next column's pages are built a few rows a
' frame; at the start of a frame register 2 shows the new page.
'
' Controls: controller 1 (or 2): the stick. On the title: up/down choose
' START or HOW TO PLAY, a button confirms.
'
' House rules: text in columns 2-29, rows 1-22; tiles from code 128; the
' frame loop is protected from the NMI; screens drawn with the NMI off
' after a wait, display off. Music first in the sound table, effects
' after; the title tune is played once. `set sprite count` is always given.

